          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56% result in a district with 44.9% college attainment, $88,902 income, and 79.8% homeownership should produce a 60%+ Republican margin based on demographic baseline alone; the 4-percentage-point underperformance is evidence of either specific governance failures, persuadable swing voters, or both — and suggests that 56% is not a stable floor but a result conditional on active incumbent performance. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 56% is a comfortable margin and no remedy is required is ignoring the evidence that current Republican positioning is leaving 4 percentage points of demographically expected support on the table." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Aging Trajectory", tag: "avoid", why: "MA-1's 58.3-year median age and stable homeowner base mean that the district's demographic future is not replacement (younger in-migration) but aging in place — a decade from now the median age will be 62–63 and the share of households dependent on Medicare and Social Security will have grown significantly. Any legislative agenda that does not anticipate and address the economic security needs of a district that is growing older, more fixed-income dependent, and more vulnerable to healthcare cost shocks is ignoring the district's most important long-term vulnerability. Ignoring this trajectory and assuming current partisan alignment will hold as household economics change is a recipe for losing this seat to a challenger who takes aging economics seriously." }
