          { name: "Education Funding and College-Educated Constituency Expectations", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.4% college attainment rate is the foundation of Diggs' 59% Democratic margin; this is a constituency that votes its values on education access, student debt management, and public school investment. Higher education affordability, K-12 funding adequacy, and the educational pipeline are not swing issues but baseline expectations that Diggs must meet visibly and concretely. Any challenger who cannot demonstrate equal or greater commitment to education as a public good will find this constituency's loyalty to Diggs reinforced, making the 59% genuinely durable on this dimension." },
          { name: "Healthcare Costs and Access for Aging Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "At 48.9-year median age with 79.3% homeownership and substantial fixed-income retirees, healthcare cost pressure and access to quality services are material concerns for a significant share of the constituency. Medicare sustainability, prescription drug costs, rural hospital viability (if applicable to coastal Barnstable), and long-term care affordability affect older homeowners' real fiscal security. This is an issue where Democratic messaging has structural advantages but where specific policy competence — not abstract commitment — determines whether the constituency believes the candidate will actually deliver relief. A challenger who can credibly speak to healthcare cost containment without privatizing Medicare or undermining universal access could create an opening here." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 margin in a prosperous, college-educated district appears safe but masks real fiscal pressures and age-related concerns that can mobilize swing voters if a challenger speaks directly to older homeowner economic security. The 41% opposition is not a fixed floor; it represents an opening if Democrats neglect fiscal competence or Republicans can credibly address healthcare and education without seeming hostile to core district values. Diggs should treat this as a district where consolidation is necessary but not sufficient — active engagement with the fiscal concerns of the homeowner majority is required to make the 59% truly durable." },
          { name: "Cultural Conservative Messaging as a Path to Persuasion", tag: "avoid", why: "At 41.4% college education and 73.25% white with a demonstrated 59% Democratic lean, MA-2 is not a district where cultural conservative messaging is likely to move voters; it is more likely to activate Democratic turnout and reinforce existing partisan boundaries. Any Republican challenger who leads with abortion restriction, religious liberty framing, or opposition to diversity programming will lose persuadable swing voters (older, fiscally concerned homeowners) and activate the college-educated base against them. The path to competitiveness in MA-2 is fiscal competence and healthcare cost management, not cultural conservatism." }
