          "However, the district's demographic profile also contains a structural warning: at 53.4 years median age and 20.8% renters, MA-03 is an aging community with a shrinking share of younger, more mobile adults. The college attainment rate of 46.8%—well above the national average but not exceptional for Massachusetts—suggests this is not a tech-hub or knowledge-economy boomtown, but rather a more traditional educated professional and public-sector workforce. For Davis and any successor, the strategic vulnerability is not the current 57% margin but what happens if younger adults with different economic priorities move elsewhere, if healthcare costs or property tax burdens become unbearable for fixed-income retirees, or if the Republican opposition develops a credible economic message on cost-of-living, housing affordability, or the tax burden on middle-class homeowners. A 57% margin in a stable, affluent district is genuinely safe today—but it rests on continued delivery to an aging constituency whose material concerns around healthcare access, property tax fairness, and the affordability of retirement in place are not permanent political facts."
