          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement & Values", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 34.7% college attainment, MA-10 has a significantly educated constituency — higher than the state baseline — whose voting behavior is increasingly driven by cultural and governance values rather than inherited party loyalty. The 53% Democratic margin in a high-income, homeowner-heavy district reflects educated suburban voters' decision to cross partisan lines, likely on grounds of institutional competence, healthcare access, education quality, and social policy. A candidate who engages college-educated voters on the specific governance issues they evaluate — public education funding, healthcare system stability, environmental quality, and institutional credibility — rather than treating education primarily as a socioeconomic marker, can consolidate and expand this coalition." },
          { name: "Understanding the 47% Republican Base", tag: "careful", why: "At 53-47, MA-10 is genuinely competitive, and the 47% Republican share reflects a constituency of affluent homeowners who are making an active choice for Republican governance despite the district's education and income profile. This is not a passive coalition — it is voters who are consciously prioritizing lower taxes, limited government, and conservative values over the education and healthcare messaging that appears to be drawing college-educated neighbors to the Democratic column. Any incumbent or challenger must understand specifically whether the 47% is concentrated among older homeowners, recent arrivals prioritizing fiscal conservatism, or business owners concerned about regulation — and address those constituents' actual concerns rather than assuming they are simply waiting to be persuaded." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 margin in a district this affluent and educated is not a comfortable hold — it is a plurality of voters in a competitive race where the opposition represents nearly half the constituency and includes substantial numbers of homeowners and college-educated professionals. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns as though 53% represents a safe majority, or who neglects the affluent suburban base's concrete economic concerns in favor of broader progressive messaging, risks finding the educated-voter coalition fragmented in the next cycle. This is a swing district that requires active engagement of the opposition's concerns, not assumption of a durable Democratic base." },
          { name: "Over-Indexing on Non-Affluent Constituent Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "MA-10's median household income of $100,670 and 23.1% renter rate mean that the district is overwhelmingly affluent and homeowner-dominant — and any candidate whose message focuses primarily on affordable housing, renters' rights, or support for lower-income households is misaligned with the material reality of most people who live here. While the renter population (likely younger professionals and empty-nesters) merits specific attention, a campaign that leads with working-class economic anxiety or frames the district's problems through a lower-income lens will struggle to resonate with the affluent majority that determines elections in MA-10. The swing voters who produced the 53-47 result are primarily concerned with their own economic security and values, not redistribution or support for populations substantially less affluent than themselves." }
