          { name: "Working-Class Wage & Economic Security Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's $61,250 median income, 19.1% college attainment (lowest peer comparison), and 37.4-year median age together identify a younger, working-class constituency that evaluates politicians on whether wages and cost of living are improving for their households. Minimum wage advocacy, worker protections, childcare affordability, and healthcare cost containment are the material governance concerns that determine whether working-class voters remain engaged or perceive the incumbent as disconnected from their economic reality. At this income and education level, rhetoric about 'supporting workers' without concrete policy delivery registers as hollow." },
          { name: "Latino & Immigrant Community Constituency Care", tag: "careful", why: "The 19.25% Hispanic/Latino population represents approximately one in five district residents, and in Massachusetts this community includes immigrant and undocumented workers whose economic vulnerability on wages, workplace protections, housing, and immigration enforcement makes them naturally aligned with Democratic policy but dependent on perceiving active incumbent advocacy for their interests. The unaccounted-for 13.3% gap likely overlaps substantially with this community, suggesting the actual minority constituency may exceed the reported Hispanic percentage. This is a community that requires ongoing, active constituent service, not assumption of support." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Seat Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result indicates the absence of Republican recruitment and relative Democratic institutional strength in the district—not invulnerability. A renter-majority, working-class constituency in a high-cost-of-living region can shift rapidly if an incumbent loses connection to housing and wage concerns, or if primary competition emerges from candidates perceived as more aggressively pursuing tenant protections and worker advocacy. The incumbent should campaign this seat with the intensity and constituent focus appropriate to a genuinely competitive district, not take Democratic lean as permanent cover for reduced legislative output." },
          { name: "Assuming Latino Support Without Active Advocacy", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 19.25% Latino population is naturally aligned with Democratic positioning on immigration and labor issues, assuming their engagement without active, visible constituent advocacy and legislative delivery on their specific concerns creates vulnerability to either reduced turnout or primary competition from candidates perceived as more aggressively serving immigrant and working-class Latino communities. In a district where one in five residents is Latino, treating this constituency as a demographic given rather than an active constituency requiring ongoing engagement is a strategic error that an unopposed 2024 result can mask but will eventually surface." }
