          "Address renter economic security directly: 45% of households rent rather than own, signaling a population vulnerable to displacement and cost-of-living pressure; housing affordability, tenant protections, and the relationship between housing costs and wage stagnation are the material economic concerns for nearly half the district's families and deserve legislative focus, not assumption.",
          "Engage working-class household economic stress: $81,149 median income in an urban Massachusetts district with rising healthcare, housing, and childcare costs means most families are managing real budget constraints; candidates who offer concrete policy answers to these material concerns rather than abstract positioning will command credibility with the constituency that actually lives here.",
          "Do not treat 100% unopposed as equivalent to 100% durable: the absence of electoral opposition reflects the strength of Democratic registration, not the absence of material grievances or the impossibility of primary challenge; any candidate who assumes the seat is safe rather than actively building constituent relationships and delivering on economic and immigrant-community concerns will find that assumption tested."
