          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Reflects Unassailable Support", tag: "avoid", why: "Reyes' 2024 unopposed victory should be read as the GOP's absence of investment in MA-4, not as affirmation that the seat is structurally Democratic. A sophisticated Republican or independent challenger who campaigns on bread-and-butter economic issues—housing affordability, wages, healthcare access—rather than partisan identity has electoral room in a district where 58% are renters and many constituents evaluate on transactional delivery rather than inherited loyalty. Complacency based on unopposed status is the path to vulnerability." },
          { name: "Generic 'Latino Outreach' Rather Than Community-Specific Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "At 67.42% Hispanic/Latino, MA-4 contains distinct communities—Puerto Rican, Dominican, Central American, Mexican-origin—with differing immigration histories, economic niches, and policy priorities that cannot be addressed through generic 'minority outreach.' A representative who treats the Latino constituency as a unified voting bloc rather than engaging specific communities' distinct concerns around economic opportunity, legal status, and cultural issues will find that engagement shallow and electorally vulnerable to a challenger who invests in community-specific relationships and demonstrates specific understanding of each group's priorities." }
