          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Permission to Neglect Material Constituent Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed 2024 victory may reflect current Democratic organizational strength and opponent weakness, but it should not be misread as voter enthusiasm or settled constituent loyalty. The district's underlying economic stress—55.5% renters, $72K income, 25.6% college attainment—creates vulnerability to any challenger who offers concrete answers to housing, wage, and healthcare concerns. An incumbent who interprets unopposed status as permission to focus on statewide or personal advancement rather than consistent constituent service will find that vulnerability rapidly exploited." },
          { name: "Defaulting to National Democratic Messaging Rather Than District-Specific Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A working-class, renter-heavy, 40% Latino district with $72K median income evaluates representation primarily on material economic delivery—housing stability, wage adequacy, healthcare access—not on alignment with national party messaging or cultural positioning. A representative who campaigns on national Democratic priorities while delivering inconsistently on district-specific housing, wage, and healthcare concerns will find that gap increasingly salient to voters facing real economic constraints. The unopposed 2024 result should be understood as an opportunity to build durable, delivery-based constituent relationships before opposition mobilizes around these material concerns." }
