          { name: "Working-Class and Cost-of-Living Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's 46.4-year median age and 68.2% non-college-educated adult population describe a significant working-class constituency whose economic concerns extend beyond housing to healthcare costs, childcare, and wage stagnation. At $90,343 median household income, the district is affluent on average, but this masks real inequality between college-educated professionals and working-class households for whom fixed costs are rising faster than incomes. A legislative agenda that addresses the material economic pressures facing working-class constituents—not as an afterthought, but as a central governing priority—better reflects the district's actual demographic composition." },
          { name: "Growing Communities of Color and Civic Representation", tag: "careful", why: "The Hispanic/Latino (14.18%), Black (4.14%), and Asian (1.92%) populations together represent over 20% of the district and are demographically growing, yet an unopposed election provides zero electoral data on whether these communities feel adequately represented, heard, and invested in the incumbent's agenda. Economic mobility, healthcare access, and education quality are the core concerns for these constituencies, and their alignment with Democratic representation depends on whether they experience responsive constituent service and legislative attention to their distinct concerns. Treating these communities as a monolithic 'minority vote' rather than engaging their specific policy priorities is a strategic vulnerability." },
          { name: "Taking Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Permanent Dominance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100-0 result is not evidence that MA-House-12th will remain Democratic indefinitely; it is evidence that there was no Republican opposition in this specific cycle. Over a five-to-ten-year horizon, demographic shifts (the growing Latino and younger-adult populations), economic pressure on renters, and generational change in political alignment could create an opening for a credible challenger if the incumbent takes the seat's security for granted. Complacency about constituent service, responsiveness to working-class concerns, and meaningful engagement with growing communities of color would be strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Assuming College-Educated Professionals Are the District's Political Core", tag: "avoid", why: "While 31.8% college attainment is above state average and describes a significant professional constituency, 68.2% of the district's adults lack a college degree—a working-class and working-middle-class majority whose economic and social concerns are distinct from those of affluent professionals. Framing the district's political identity around college-educated professionals, higher education funding, and progressive cultural issues risks neglecting the material economic concerns of the larger non-college-educated constituency. A representative who leads with working-class economic security rather than assuming college-educated professionals will automatically provide the core vote better reflects the district's actual demographic composition." }
