          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 8.76% of the district, the Hispanic community represents approximately 1,050-1,100 residents and is large enough to meaningfully shift a race decided by a 59-41 margin. In a suburban district with $84,555 median income and strong educational institutions, Hispanic households are likely concentrated in service-sector, skilled trades, and professional roles where economic mobility, housing affordability, and education pathways are the primary concerns. Authentic engagement with Hispanic small business owners, trades workers, and families—particularly around education quality and housing costs—builds a constituency relationship that can grow over time." },
          { name: "The 41% Opposition Base", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this affluent and educated—median age 40.3, college-educated at 33.3%—a 41% Democratic opposition is a meaningful and organized constituency that cannot be ignored or dismissed. This cohort likely includes younger renters (28.4%), educated professionals, and families concerned with progressive governance priorities; whether motivated by education spending, climate policy, or social issues, they represent real constituent demand that an incumbent operating at 59% cannot treat as safely consolidated. Understanding specifically what drives the 41%—and whether any portion represents persuadable voters rather than locked opposition—is essential for holding the seat in a more competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Running on the Margin Rather Than Governing the Whole District", tag: "avoid", why: "At 59%, Pease holds a solid margin but one that depends on retaining the affluent homeowner base and avoiding significant erosion among the 41% opposition. A representative who campaigns and governs as though the 59% is a sufficient mandate—ignoring the concerns of renters, younger adults, or the Hispanic community—risks the kind of base fatigue that can convert a 59-41 result into a competitive race when demographic or political conditions shift. The 40.3 median age and 28.4% renter rate signal a district with generational turnover and non-traditional housing arrangements that reward active constituent engagement, not passive majority assumption." },
          { name: "Treating Affluence as a Proxy for Partisan Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "Upper-middle-class suburban districts with $84,555 income and 33.3% college attainment do not automatically reward partisan loyalty—they reward competent governance, fiscal responsibility, and delivery on education and infrastructure. In Massachusetts, suburban affluent constituencies have become increasingly skeptical of partisan rigidity and more likely to vote based on specific policy performance and local representative responsiveness. Any incumbent who assumes that income and education automatically translate to safe re-election margins, rather than treating them as constituencies that demand evidence of constituent service and fiscal competence, risks losing the core coalition that currently produces the 59% result." }
