          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing & Cost-of-Living Crisis", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 77.6%, MA-10 has the highest renter rate of any district analyzed in this sequence and a median household income of $29,373—placing the vast majority of the district's constituents in precarious housing situations where rent consumes an unsustainable percentage of income. Eviction prevention, rent stabilization, tenant protections, and affordable housing development are not abstract policy issues but survival concerns for three-quarters of the district's households. Young adults (median age 31.2) and families with children in renting households evaluate their representative on whether government is actively protecting their housing security; neglecting this issue cedes electoral territory to any challenger who addresses it directly." },
          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Community Cohesion Amid Demographic Diversity", tag: "careful", why: "While Hispanic/Latino residents comprise 65.78% of the district, the remaining 34.22% is distributed across Black (17.43%), White (15.62%), and Asian (3.13%) communities—plus an ~3.82% gap likely representing indigenous or multiracial residents. At these proportions, the district is neither a homogeneous Hispanic enclave nor a fully integrated multicultural space, but rather a community where Hispanic/Latino political dominance is real and other communities' distinct economic and social concerns risk being subsumed into a single narrative. A representative who acknowledges the Black community's distinct economic mobility barriers, the White community's economic anxieties, and the Asian community's specific workforce and small-business concerns—while centering Hispanic/Latino majority leadership—better serves the actual constituency and prevents resentment that could fragment the coalition." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Reflects Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result reflects the current political environment in MA-10, not an immutable electoral floor. A district with 65.78% Hispanic/Latino residents at $29,373 median income, 77.6% renter rate, and median age of 31.2 is, in structural terms, a working-class young constituency facing real economic pressure—precisely the demographic that responds to credible alternatives when they appear. Treating an unopposed victory as evidence of unshakeable incumbency rather than an opening to deepen constituent engagement is the error that produces primary challengers and competitive general elections." },
          { name: "Abstracting District Issues as 'Progressive' vs. 'Conservative' Ideology", tag: "avoid", why: "MA-10's constituency is not politically motivated by ideological frameworks but by survival economics: keeping a roof over their heads, accessing healthcare, finding jobs that pay enough to live on, and protecting their children's futures. A representative who frames housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity as progressive ideology rather than as material constituent needs—or who dismisses these concerns as outside the scope of state legislative authority—will find the district's political loyalty is contingent on whether their representative is actually delivering for them. The unopposed 2024 result should not be mistaken for ideological alignment; it reflects satisfaction with constituent service and responsiveness to real economic concerns." }
