          { name: "Treating 73% as a Safe Majority Rather Than an Earned Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% result in an educated, engaged suburban district is not a floor — it is a current preference among highly-informed voters who evaluate their representative on concrete delivery of education quality, housing affordability, and community investment. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though the 73% is stable and defensive rather than actively maintained through genuine constituent service and alignment with the district's material priorities will find that margin at risk. The 27% opposition in this district likely includes educated voters with specific disagreements on education policy, housing development, or environmental protection — not disengaged voters who can be ignored." },
          { name: "Abstracting from the District's Material Concerns to Partisan Ideology", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58.2% college-educated, $119,955-median-income constituency that is 38% non-white will evaluate a representative primarily on whether they are addressing education quality, housing affordability, infrastructure investment, and environmental protection — not on partisan positioning or abstract ideology. Any candidate whose messaging defaults to national partisan frames rather than district-specific governance priorities misses the fundamental reality of an educated electorate that votes based on material concerns and local performance. In a district this affluent and informed, district-specific expertise and constituent attention command more credibility than partisan rhetoric." }
