          { name: "Asian Plurality Community Engagement & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 30.66%, Asian residents comprise the largest single demographic group in the district and are not a monolithic constituency. The plurality may include East Asian professionals, South Asian immigrants, Southeast Asian families, or mixed communities with distinct economic concerns, cultural institutions, and voting behavior. A representative who engages this plurality with institutional specificity — understanding whether priorities are education policy, small-business access to capital, or workforce pipeline development — builds durable support in the group most likely to swing if they perceive a competitor is more responsive to their actual needs." },
          { name: "Young Adult Economic Mobility & Urban Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 32.9-year median age is young for Massachusetts, and this cohort is evaluating whether they can afford to remain in the district, access educational and job opportunity, and build a stable future. The 78% Democratic result reflects current alignment, but in a district where renters and younger adults predominate, economic stagnation or visible decay in job markets and neighborhood vitality is a vulnerability. This constituency is pragmatic rather than ideologically committed; a challenger who can credibly promise job growth and affordability could mobilize the 22% opposition and shift persuadable younger voters." },
          { name: "Positioning Renter-Focused Policy as Anti-Business or Class Warfare", tag: "avoid", why: "The $72,782 median income and 33.1% college attainment signal a professional and working-class constituency with economic stakes in both neighborhood vitality and small-business health. Framing housing affordability policy as zero-sum conflict between renters and business owners concedes the frame that a Democratic candidate should reject. Equitable housing policy that includes small-business support, job creation, and neighborhood economic development serves both renters' immediate economic security and the district's broader economic health." },
          { name: "Taking the 78% Margin as Durable or Treating Opposition as Residual", tag: "avoid", why: "A 78-22 result in a district this young (32.9 median age), this diverse (no group above 36%), and this economically dependent on renter stability (66.6%) is not a landslide — it is a coalition that holds if the incumbent delivers on housing, transit, job access, and school quality. The 22% opposition is not ideologically fixed; it likely includes white homeowners skeptical of renter-focused policy, business owners frustrated with regulation, and persuadable Asian and Latino voters who evaluate on concrete delivery. Treating this as a safe seat rather than an active governance responsibility is the fastest path to vulnerability." }
