          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Equity in a Diverse, Affluent District", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 28.75% Black, 8.77% Asian, and 7.42% Hispanic residents, MA-7th Norfolk is genuinely diverse despite its overall affluence and Democratic alignment. These communities are not monolithic, and their presence in the district is large enough that specific engagement on equity issues — economic mobility barriers, educational disparities, workplace equity, and targeted community investment — is both a governance obligation and a source of Democratic electoral strength. Treating demographic diversity as automatic support rather than as communities with specific policy concerns risks losing votes to candidates who engage more authentically on these issues." },
          { name: "Complacency Risk in a 64-Point Margin", tag: "careful", why: "A 64-36 result is a commanding margin, but it reflects 36% of the district actively opposing the incumbent — a significant base for a challenger to build on if the incumbent treats the margin as permission to coast rather than as a mandate for continued engagement. In a district this affluent and this diverse, sustained constituent service, responsiveness to specific policy concerns, and authentic relationship-building with minority communities are what maintain Democratic dominance; absence of these activities is what creates vulnerability despite a large current margin." },
          { name: "Partisan Rhetoric Over Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "High-income, college-educated professionals evaluate their representatives primarily on constituent service effectiveness, responsiveness to specific economic concerns, and legislative competence — not on partisan messaging or ideological purity. An incumbent who substitutes campaign rhetoric for actual engagement on healthcare costs, education financing, or property tax policy will find that professionals, who have options and high expectations for government responsiveness, shift their support to a challenger who delivers more tangibly." },
          { name: "Treating Minority Communities as Guaranteed Democratic Votes", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Black, Asian, and Hispanic residents together comprise 44.94% of the population, assuming their support without specific engagement on their distinct economic and social concerns is strategically dangerous. Minority professionals in a high-income district have specific mobility concerns, equity expectations, and service demands that differ from white professionals; a representative who engages these communities only during election cycles risks losing votes to candidates who build sustained relationships and offer more targeted policy responsiveness." }
