          { name: "Racial Equity and Community-Specific Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "With Black residents comprising 33.89% of the district, Latino residents 14.47%, and white residents 30.32%, MA-House-10th is a genuinely diverse community where no single racial group forms a natural majority. The unopposed 2024 result provides no data on whether the incumbent is serving these communities with equal attention or whether Latino and Black constituents perceive themselves as genuinely represented versus treated as dependent on Democratic default. Building specific relationships with Black and Latino community organizations, addressing distinct concerns around police accountability, economic opportunity, and cultural recognition, and delivering measurable outcomes in each community creates the kind of durable constituent attachment that makes an unopposed victory the result of genuine support rather than opposition default." },
          { name: "Understanding Why the 2024 Unopposed Status Occurred", tag: "careful", why: "The 100-0 partisan result in 2024 is an unusual electoral outcome in Massachusetts that could reflect either weakness in the Republican Party's ability to recruit candidates in urban/inner-suburban districts or the incumbent's personal strength and constituent relationship. Understanding which explanation is accurate — and whether the unopposed status is durable or vulnerable — is critical for determining whether the incumbent can rely on similar 2024 conditions in the next cycle or whether a credible challenger will emerge. If the unopposed status reflects Republican party weakness rather than Dubois's personal strength, a future well-recruited candidate could shift the margin significantly." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Victory as a Mandate Rather Than an Anomaly", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election produces no information about whether constituents actually prefer the incumbent or simply lack an alternative; treating a 100-0 result as evidence of durable support is strategically dangerous because it provides no feedback on which issues matter, how effectively the incumbent is serving distinct communities, or what vulnerabilities a credible challenger could exploit. The absence of opposition is not a sign of strength but the absence of information — and the incumbent's strategic task is to build the kind of explicit constituent relationship and measurable governance outcomes that would produce a genuine majority even in a competitive race." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty as a Substitute for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "At 37.7% renter rate and $80,463 median income, MA-House-10th's constituents are experiencing real economic pressure that does not respond to partisan messaging; housing cost, public safety, and access to opportunity are material concerns that require specific legislative response rather than generic Democratic positioning. Any candidate who consolidates the assumed Democratic base without demonstrating concrete progress on the material concerns of working-class and lower-middle-class constituents risks losing that base if a credible challenger emerges with a specific agenda and genuine constituent relationships in Black, Latino, and working-class neighborhoods." }
