          { name: "Immigrant Community Service and Language Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The ~24.84% demographic gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents a substantial community—likely including Cape Verdean, Portuguese, and other immigrant populations with deep historical ties to southeastern Massachusetts. Constituent service that requires language access, immigration policy advocacy, workforce development tailored to immigrant workers, and culturally informed engagement with community organizations is not peripheral—it is essential representation for a quarter of the district. A representative perceived as disconnected from these communities or unwilling to invest in language access and culturally competent outreach is underserving a core constituency." },
          { name: "Economic Vulnerability of Younger, Working-Class Families", tag: "careful", why: "The 33.1 median age combined with 56.7% renter rate, $67,014 income, and 20.1% college attainment describes a constituency of young families managing housing costs, childcare expenses, and wage work without substantial credential-based mobility. Childcare affordability, school quality, living wage employment, and access to training pathways are the issues most likely to drive electoral mobilization if a representative is perceived as inattentive. However, this constituency is also the most likely to face competing demands on time and attention—making constituent outreach and service infrastructure essential to maintain connection." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Reflects Voter Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result reflects the district's deep Democratic alignment and lack of organized Republican challenge, not active satisfaction with the incumbent's constituent service or legislative record. In a district this economically pressured, unopposed status can mask constituent frustration with housing affordability, healthcare access, or school quality that would surface immediately if a credible alternative emerged. Resting on unopposed margins rather than actively demonstrating material gains on behalf of constituents is a path to vulnerability if circumstances change." },
          { name: "Treating Race and Immigration as Secondary Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 45.71% Black, 12.99% Hispanic, and includes a substantial ~24.84% gap representing immigrant communities, a representative who frames racial equity, immigrant rights, and community safety as secondary to other issues is not reflecting constituent priorities. Policing practices, school discipline equity, housing discrimination, and immigration enforcement are issues that directly affect the material wellbeing of this district's majority-minority constituency. Any legislative record or constituent service approach that deprioritizes these issues relative to abstract state-level concerns risks alienating the core constituency." }
