          { name: "Allowing Housing, Education, and Childcare Issues to Remain Abstract Rather Than Delivering Visible Results", tag: "avoid", why: "A district of young, college-educated renters facing severe housing and education affordability pressures expects concrete legislative outcomes — not abstract progressive positioning — on the issues that constrain their economic security and life possibilities. Michlewitz's next challenge will come not from the right but from a potential progressive challenger who can point to specific housing units created, childcare subsidies expanded, or tenant protections enacted as concrete evidence of governance competence in a district where these issues are the material foundation of constituent wellbeing. Rhetorical commitment to housing affordability without delivering results creates political vulnerability to a challenger with a more aggressive legislative record." }
