          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Evidence of Deep Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Democratic result in the absence of Republican opposition does not indicate that the incumbent has solved the district's material problems—it indicates that no Republican mounted a challenge. The district's 60.2% renter rate, $70,440 median income, and 23.3% college attainment indicate a working-class constituency facing real economic pressures whose support for the incumbent is contingent on measurable delivery on housing, economic mobility, and community investment. An incumbent who mistakes unopposed status for security and reduces constituent engagement or legislative focus on these core concerns is creating conditions for primary challenge or organized opposition." },
          { name: "Assuming the South End's Electoral Alignment Is Durable Without Addressing Gentrification Pressure", tag: "avoid", why: "The South End's historical role as a gateway neighborhood for immigrant and working-class communities is under sustained pressure from gentrification and real estate speculation; the 60.2% renter rate indicates that most constituents lack ownership protection against displacement, and the median income of $70,440 is insufficient to compete with market-rate housing costs in an increasingly expensive Boston neighborhood. An incumbent who does not center anti-displacement and permanently affordable housing policy as a legislative priority is allowing the district's demographic character and constituent base to be reshaped by market forces; eventually, this creates conditions where the district's electoral behavior changes because the constituency itself has changed. This is preventable only through active anti-displacement housing policy—not later, after gentrification is complete." }
