          "Build active constituent infrastructure in the Black majority community (58%) with specific attention to economic mobility, workforce development, and equitable access to jobs in growing sectors — unopposed margins create complacency, and organized attention to Black community economic concerns by any future challenger represents genuine electoral vulnerability.",
          "Engage the Hispanic (15%) and white (18%) communities as distinct constituencies with specific needs rather than subsuming them into a monolithic 'minority coalition' narrative — service-sector economic concerns for Hispanic workers, neighborhood stability for white residents, and equitable resource distribution across all three communities are the foundation of durable multi-ethnic governance.",
          "Treat the unopposed 100% result as a governance obligation, not an electoral guarantee: the absence of Republican opposition does not mean the absence of constituent concerns or potential future challengers; active, visible constituent service and responsive attention to district economic concerns are the only reliable buffer against primary or general-election vulnerability."
