          { name: "Mixed-Tenure Housing and Cost-of-Living Squeeze", tag: "careful", why: "The 37.1% renter rate signals a significant population of renters alongside the 62.9% homeowner majority — a constituency increasingly exposed to rising housing costs in a high-cost Massachusetts market. Renters and working-class homeowners face different but overlapping pressures: renters need affordability and protection from displacement; homeowners need property tax management and service quality. The incumbent's record on housing policy, rent control, zoning reform, and affordability programs directly affects whether this mixed-tenure coalition stays consolidated around the Democratic incumbent or whether a portion (particularly younger renters) becomes persuadable to an alternative offering on cost of living." },
          { name: "Assuming Democratic Registration as an Electoral Guarantee", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a district with 32.3% college education and $81,556 median income — solidly Democratic-leaning demographics by Massachusetts standards — is not a reflection of unshakeable party loyalty; it is the result of the incumbent's personal performance and constituent perception of whether he is delivering on their priorities. The 43% opposition is not an insignificant base of immovable Republicans; it represents voters in a district where Democratic registration should produce a higher margin, meaning there is specific dissatisfaction or defection risk. Any incumbent who campaigns on the assumption that being a Democrat in a Democratic-leaning district is sufficient will miss the actual work required to build a durable majority." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safe Because of Democratic Lean", tag: "avoid", why: "Massachusetts House districts are not immune to candidate-specific performance or to shifts in constituent priorities, and a 57% result — while solid — is not a supermajority margin in a district where unemployment, cost-of-living pressures, and service quality failures could quickly mobilize opposition. The combination of 37.1% renters in a high-cost market, 17.14% Latino population potentially mobilizable by an alternative candidate, and working-class homeowners sensitive to property tax and service issues means this district is more volatile than the partisan lean alone suggests. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 57-43 as a stable floor rather than a result that must be actively earned and defended will find the race tighter than expected." }
