          { name: "Treating 81.84% White Plurality as Sufficient Electoral Base", tag: "avoid", why: "An incumbent who consolidates only the white homeowner plurality in a district with 18.16% minority population and 26.4% renters is ceding the two constituencies most likely to swing elections based on dissatisfaction with housing costs, school investment, or economic opportunity. In a high-income Massachusetts district, minority communities and renters are not sideline constituencies—they are present, organized, and capable of evaluating on whether government is delivering for them. An unopposed incumbent who has not systematically engaged these groups leaves them available as a challenger's primary recruitment base." }
