          { name: "Asian Community Integration & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 25.7%, the Asian population represents one-quarter of the district — a demographic cohort large enough to represent a distinct constituency with specific economic and social concerns, yet one that is frequently rendered invisible in generic district analysis. The Asian community in Worcester's high-income, college-educated neighborhoods includes professionals in healthcare, technology, and education whose families have invested substantially in local economic opportunity and whose concerns about discrimination, housing access, and educational equity are direct material interests. A representative who engages the Asian community with specificity — not as a 'diversity' checkbox, but as a primary constituent group with concrete policy stakes in healthcare access, education quality, and economic mobility — builds the durable relationship that the district's demographic composition demands." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Rental Market Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "At 28% renters and $140,610 median income, this is a district where housing costs are likely consuming a substantial share of household budgets even among relatively affluent residents — suggesting both younger professionals building toward homeownership and established residents downsizing into rental housing. The tension between high income and high cost-of-living in Massachusetts means that even $140K household incomes may struggle with regional housing costs, particularly for the 28% renting. A representative who acknowledges this tension without dismissing it as a luxury problem, and who engages renters' and first-time buyers' specific concerns around zoning, development, and affordability policy, avoids the electoral vulnerability of appearing tone-deaf to a constituency segment that exists within this affluent district." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Affluence as Monolithic Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The fact that Kane won unopposed in 2024 should not obscure the reality that no district is actually monolithic, and a 66.4% college-educated, $140K-income constituency is still diverse in its actual policy priorities and governance expectations. The unopposed result may reflect genuine consensus around the incumbent, or it may reflect insufficient challenge recruitment — but either way, it is a baseline that provides no information about where genuine opposition or persuadable voters might emerge if a credible challenger mounted a campaign on specific issues like tax policy, education funding, housing affordability, or healthcare access." },
          { name: "Neglecting Lower-Income Minority Constituents", tag: "avoid", why: "While the district is 60.97% white and 25.7% Asian, the remaining 12% includes Hispanic, Black, and other communities whose economic circumstances may differ substantially from the district's median of $140,610 and whose policy concerns — immigration enforcement, healthcare access, education equity, police accountability — may be distinct from the majority's. Assuming that high-income district demographics mean all constituents share identical policy priorities risks rendering these communities politically invisible and creates an opening for a challenger who engages them with specificity." }
