          { name: "Ideological Projects at the Expense of Local Problem-Solving", tag: "avoid", why: "High-income, college-educated constituencies in Massachusetts are ideologically diverse and evaluate representatives primarily on constituent service and local governance results, not ideological alignment. An incumbent who becomes identified with statewide ideological projects at the expense of local school funding, property tax fairness, or community infrastructure maintenance will find even this loyal constituency opening to a challenger who credibly promises to focus on local problem-solving. Unopposed status can create the psychological trap that the incumbent has a mandate for a broader agenda; actually, the district has granted only a conditional mandate for local competence that must be continuously renewed through constituent service." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Hispanic Minority Community (10.24%)", tag: "avoid", why: "At 10.24% of the district population, the Hispanic community is large enough — roughly 8,000-10,000 residents — to swing a future competitive race if mobilized around specific concerns (economic opportunity, education access, healthcare) or united around a challenger who offers more direct engagement. In a district currently defined by white, affluent homeowner priorities, the Hispanic community can become invisible in constituent attention and policy development; this invisibility is a strategic vulnerability because it creates an opening for a future challenger who offers specific representation and responsiveness to this growing constituency's distinct concerns." }
