          "Serve the 12.59% Hispanic/Latino and 7.23% Black communities with specific constituent attention and visible policy record—in a district where white college-educated professionals dominate the narrative, communities of color can become invisible; immigration policy, healthcare access equity, and economic opportunity are the issues that signal whether the incumbent is governing the whole district or just the demographic majority.",
          "Engage the education and healthcare sectors specifically—51.7% college attainment and median age 34.4 signal substantial employment in schools, universities, and healthcare systems; education funding adequacy, student debt policy, and healthcare workforce compensation are employer-side and worker-side concerns that deserve legislative attention and visibility.",
          "Do not treat a 100% unopposed result as permission to reduce constituent contact—unopposed elections in Massachusetts at the state legislative level have created vulnerabilities when incumbents become personally unpopular or when economic conditions shift; active town halls, visible constituent services, and regular communication with renters and communities of color are the baseline for holding this seat in a genuinely competitive future cycle."
