          "Lead with Hispanic/Latino constituent engagement and economic mobility: at 30.36%, this is the district's largest demographic group, and their concerns around housing affordability, wage stagnation, and healthcare access are the material issues most likely to determine turnout and candidate preference. Specific legislative focus on these concerns, not generic minority outreach, is the foundation for holding this seat.",
          "Address the renter majority's economic security directly: at 58.6% renters and $57,686 income, the district's households are economically constrained and more responsive to direct service delivery and cost-of-living issues than to abstract political messaging. Tenant protections, affordable housing production, and living wage policy are the practical governance issues that will sustain or erode Democratic consolidation.",
          "Monitor for insurgent primary or general-election challengers: without established Republican opposition, the most likely threat to this seat in the near term is either a Democratic primary challenger positioned to the left on economic issues, or a Republican recruitment effort targeting a candidate who can articulate economic security messaging to working-class renters and Hispanic/Latino voters. Prepare for both."
