          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed victory in 2024 reflects temporary opposition weakness, not durable district alignment. Massachusetts' political environment has produced credible Democratic challengers in districts with similar demographics, and the next cycle will likely produce a challenger who enters with an explicit plan to mobilize Hispanic residents, renters, and college-educated voters on affordable housing and education access. Resting on the unopposed result rather than building constituent infrastructure and delivering governance performance invites surprise competition." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Voters as Inherited Partisans", tag: "avoid", why: "The 34.9% college-attainment rate in an affluent district ($105,749 income) describes an educated, economically secure constituency that votes on governance performance and policy specificity, not partisan inheritance. Massachusetts' college-educated voters have demonstrated willingness to shift support based on education policy, environmental regulation, and healthcare-access performance. A representative who assumes educated homeowners will remain loyal regardless of legislative results on these issues will find that assumption tested in a competitive cycle." }
