          "Build a specific housing strategy that addresses both the homeowner-majority's property tax and school-funding concerns and the renter-quarter's affordability and displacement concerns; in a $150k-income district, both constituencies are present and neither is peripheral. Framing housing as a single-axis issue (either pro-development or pro-affordability) rather than managing both tensions simultaneously will hand a challenger an opening with whichever constituency feels neglected.",
          "Maintain active constituent engagement with the 23% Republican opposition as a legitimate political community, not a residual or marginal voice; these are college-educated, affluent voters making deliberate choices. Understanding specifically what issues drive their opposition — whether tax policy, education philosophy, social regulation, or something else — and engaging those concerns substantively (even while disagreeing) is more electorally durable than dismissing them as ideologically distant or unpersuadable.",
