          { name: "Economic Competence & Cost-of-Living Credibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "A $98,230-income, 44.6% college-educated constituency evaluates elected officials primarily on economic competence and whether government is delivering on material quality-of-life concerns: housing costs, childcare affordability, tax burden, and healthcare access. The Democratic incumbent's advantage is built on social progressivism, but that advantage erodes if the incumbent appears indifferent to the cost pressures that professional households face or if positioned as advocating for spending increases without fiscal discipline. Specific legislative achievements on housing affordability, childcare subsidies, and tax-code fairness — areas where Democratic values and fiscal responsibility align — are the messaging foundation that keeps affluent professionals voting Democratic despite reservations about party spending." },
          { name: "Professional & Business Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 33% Republican base in MA-Senate-2 includes business-focused professionals, entrepreneurs, and professionals skeptical of progressive taxation or regulation — a constituency that, while outnumbered, represents a meaningful share of the district's affluent professional core. This group is not inherently unreachable; chambers of commerce, professional associations, and business-sector constituents often care more about predictable, competent governance than partisan label. The incumbent should actively cultivate relationships with business leaders and professional associations not to flip them, but to ensure they perceive the incumbent as reasonable on economic policy and not reflexively hostile to business interests — reducing the chance they mobilize against the incumbent in a close cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 67% as an Invulnerable Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% result in a presidential-year election is not a mandate for maximum progressive ambition or a signal that the incumbent has indefinite political capital. It reflects high Democratic enthusiasm in a favorable year, strong base turnout, and a favorable national environment — conditions that are not guaranteed to repeat in state legislative cycles or off-year elections. Any incumbent who responds to a 67% win by interpreting it as permission to pursue aggressively progressive legislative agendas, ignore business-community concerns, or take base turnout for granted is misreading the constituency and setting up for a vulnerability in a lower-turnout cycle." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 33% as a Permanent Minority", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 33% Republican share is clearly the minority position in MA-Senate-2, that constituency includes real voters with legitimate concerns about business-friendly policy, fiscal responsibility, and pragmatic governance. Dismissing this group as irredeemable Trump supporters or ideological opponents, rather than as constituent neighbors with different policy priorities, is both a governance failure and a political vulnerability — it forecloses the possibility of persuasion and ensures that any credible Republican challenger will have a defined audience of motivated voters. Treating the 33% as people with real concerns — even disagreement — rather than as opponents to be defeated maintains legislative credibility and reduces the chance that the Republican base becomes energized." }
