          { name: "Assuming Affluence Eliminates Economic Inequality as a Governing Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "The $94,840 median income is genuinely affluent, but it coexists with a 36.5% renter rate that concentrates economic vulnerability on a substantial minority, and a 26.79% Latino population that likely includes lower-wage workers in service and care sectors. Framing the district as uniformly affluent and therefore beyond economic inequality concerns is both factually wrong and strategically dangerous — it alienates renters and immigrant workers who experience material economic pressure daily, and it leaves the incumbent vulnerable to opposition messaging that speaks directly to the cost-of-living reality for families earning $50,000-$80,000 in a high-cost state." }
