          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing Affordability & Tenant Protection Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 54% renters — a plurality that likely includes large concentrations of Latino, Black, and immigrant working-class families — at $50,199 median household income in Massachusetts' high-cost rental market, housing affordability and tenant protection are the material economic security concerns that constitute the district's primary policy agenda. Rent regulation, tenant rights enforcement, eviction prevention, and habitability standards are concrete legislative issues that directly affect the majority of Hampden households. A representative who can point to specific legislative wins on tenant protection or housing accessibility demonstrates the constituent relationship that makes opposition challenge difficult; one perceived as absent from this terrain is vulnerable." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Anxiety & Wage-Worker Constituency Service", tag: "careful", why: "The 31.72% White population, modest $50,199 income, 54% renter rate, and 18.1% college attainment together describe a multi-racial working-class constituency that may feel economic anxiety around wage stagnation, inflation, and job security independent of partisan affiliation. While the district has consolidated behind Democratic representation, a credible challenger who leads on working-class economic concerns — wages, small-business support, healthcare cost containment, school funding — could create genuine competition if the incumbent is perceived as inattentive to these material needs. This is not necessarily a partisan vulnerability, but a competence and attentiveness vulnerability that any incumbent should monitor and address proactively." },
          { name: "Treating a 100% Result as License to Neglect Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed elections in otherwise politically dynamic states often reflect tactical decisions by minority parties rather than genuine lock-in of the seat. A representative who interprets Gomez's 2024 unopposed status as permission to reduce constituent engagement, redirect resources to other districts, or become inattentive to district-specific concerns creates exactly the opening through which a credible challenger could emerge in a subsequent cycle. The unopposed result is a starting point for consolidation, not an endpoint." },
          { name: "Allowing Immigration Enforcement or Immigrant Community Concerns to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "With 47% Latino population in a district where many households are likely immigrant or immigrant-descended, immigration enforcement accountability, family separation prevention, and immigrant-rights advocacy are constituent concerns that cannot be absent from the district's legislative agenda without creating space for opposition. A representative perceived as insufficient on immigration advocacy or inattentive to ICE enforcement and deportation risk in the community invites challenge from a more aggressive immigrant-rights candidate, potentially from a different party. This issue must remain active and visible in constituent communication." }
