          "Target the 32.9% renter population as a real dissatisfaction vector — younger professionals in a $144.7K income district who cannot afford single-family homes represent a constituency that may be receptive to a challenger focused on housing supply, zoning reform, and the cost of living relative to professional salaries. This is not a poverty argument but a middle-class economic mobility argument.",
          "Engage the Asian (12.6%) and Hispanic (10.17%) professional populations specifically on whether they feel included in the district's civic institutions and whether governance is delivering for their economic security — do not assume their Democratic alignment is immovable if the incumbent is perceived as taking them for granted or not delivering measurably on their priorities.",
