          { name: "Running as the Only Candidate Without Serious Opposition Research", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed 2024 result should not create complacency; it reflects the current absence of a Republican or independent challenger with viable funding and organization, not structural invulnerability. Any challenger — particularly a moderate Republican or well-funded independent in a district with $172,355 income and 83.1% college education — would have strong targeting opportunities against an incumbent who becomes identifiable with ideological left-flank priorities at odds with the district's economic conservatism and high-income tax sensitivity. Governance and legislative record should be built to survive scrutiny from a serious challenger, not optimized only for the current unopposed position." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Loyalty Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 16.53% Asian American population and 37.2% renter rate together represent nearly 40% of the district in communities whose electoral behavior cannot be assumed; they require active constituent service, policy attention, and representation to maintain support. In a district where 100% of the current partisan vote is consolidated on one side, the strategically important work is not consolidation but the prevention of cross-pressure or defection among constituencies who could plausibly support a credible moderate challenger. Treating these communities as settled while focusing legislative energy elsewhere is a vulnerability in a district where the only competitive threat would come from a candidate who makes specific outreach to overlooked constituencies central to their campaign." }
