          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 62.7% renter rate in a district where median household income is $100,635, this is a constituency vulnerable to housing cost shocks despite above-median absolute income. Renters at this income level are typically young professionals, families with children, and workers in service and knowledge sectors — all facing real pressure from rising rents, limited affordable inventory, and neighborhood displacement. Tenant protections, rent stabilization, zoning reform that increases housing supply, and anti-displacement programs are not abstract Democratic positions in this district; they are the material governance issues that explain the 70% Democratic margin. Any candidate who becomes inattentive to housing affordability or assumes renter voters will default to Democrats creates an opening." },
          { name: "Latino Community Economic Mobility and Workplace Rights", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 30.56% of the district population, the Latino community is the second-largest demographic group and large enough to materially affect election outcomes in lower-profile races where statewide margins do not fully apply. The combination of younger median age (35.6), high renter rate (62.7%), and $100,635 income suggests a working-class and professional Latino constituency evaluating candidates on economic opportunity, housing costs, and workplace protections — not on inherited partisan loyalty. Workplace rights enforcement, immigration policy, rent stabilization, and pathways to economic mobility are the issues that consolidate Latino support; a candidate who defaults to rhetoric without delivering on these material concerns opens the door to a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Urban Neighborhood Services and Quality of Life", tag: "careful", why: "This is an urban or inner-suburban district with a young (35.6 median age), educated (47.5% college), renter-majority (62.7%) constituency that depends on public services — transit, schools, parks, public safety, trash collection, street maintenance — for daily quality of life. The 70% Democratic margin reflects satisfaction with Democratic urban governance, but that satisfaction is contingent on service delivery actually functioning. A Democratic incumbent who becomes inattentive to neighborhood conditions, street maintenance, public safety, or transit reliability gives a Republican challenger an opening to argue for more effective administration and management — an argument that can resonate in an affluent urban district if service quality has degraded." },
          { name: "Anti-Immigrant Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 30.56% of the population is Latino and 62.7% are renters (a renter-majority district with significant immigrant working-class population), anti-immigrant framing alienates the second-largest demographic group and directly contradicts the economic and social priorities of the majority. Any candidate who leads with immigration restriction, workplace enforcement against immigrants, or nativist rhetoric is writing off a constituency large enough to determine election outcomes and signaling misalignment with the material economic interests of renter-majority urban neighborhoods." },
          { name: "Assuming the 70% Margin Is Durable Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 70-30 result reflects structural Democratic alignment based on renter demographics, college education, and younger age — but it is not infinite or immune to inattention. A Democratic incumbent who takes the base for granted, becomes inattentive to housing costs, workplace rights, or neighborhood quality, or fails to actively maintain relationships with the Latino community creates an opening that should not exist in a structurally Democratic district. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 70% is a safe foundation without understanding the material conditions that produce it is underestimating the volatility of a young, renter-majority, economically mobile constituency." }
